-- Bite 6 revision, Codex, 2026-10-21, prompt-bite-6.md at 2859815:
-- Int's ring and String's monoid now live in core.lut; the remaining numeric models follow.
-- Bite 8, Claude Opus 5.5, 2026-10-21: the computed distance's `MetricWithin` claim.
-- numbers_model.lut: the core's numbers as models of algebra.lth and numbers.lth, the structure
-- of `Real` as an axiom, and the elementary functions as routes to the host's library.
--
-- Draft 0, 2026-10-14, by Claude Fable 5.1, an agent of the coordinating session, on the brief
-- record/IA/slice-theories-2026-10-14/prompt.md (item 1 of the inventory's §6). A model file
-- (12.S23): each value names which structure a core type or operation is, and so gives the
-- operations of core.lut their laws (kernel_models.lut does the same for the core's data). The
-- axiom is allowed here by 11.R3 (core and the libraries). The externs are routes (3.S40,
-- 10.S8): Python's `math`, which is what v0.1's real_python.lut bound (tag v0.1-final).

import "algebra.lth" CompleteOrderedField
import "numbers.lth" Doubles IntegerDivision Conversion Elementary absDistance abs

-- Int's quotient and remainder are the host's floor division (numbers.lth §3)
integerDivision : IntegerDivision := { quotient := fn a b { a / b }, remainder := fn a b { a mod b } }

-- `Real` is the complete ordered field: assumed, since nothing computes its operations (11.R3,
-- 11.S4); its laws carry the grade `assumed`
realField : CompleteOrderedField Real := axiom "the real numbers: the complete ordered field"

-- `Rational` is an ordered field, exactly (11.S4); its model waits for rational.lut, which
-- gives its values (`fromInt`, `ratio`, `toQ`): a `Rational` has no literal to write `0` with

-- `Float64`: the machine's operations and order (11.S4, numbers.lth §2)
doubles : Doubles Float64 := {
  plus := fn a b { a + b }, minus := fn a b { a - b }, times := fn a b { a * b }, divide := fn a b { a / b },
  less := fn a b { a < b }, leq := fn a b { a <= b }, greater := fn a b { a > b }, geq := fn a b { a >= b },
  unequal := fn a b { a /= b },
  epsilon := 2.220446049250313e-16,
}

-- the doubles' claim, at `Float64` (execution.md section 4; the coordinator's (a) of 2026-10-22):
-- `plus` and `times` of a `Float64` resolve to `doubles`' fields, with numbers.lth's laws as their
-- evidence; the doubles are not a semiring (11.S15)
DoublesHaveTheOperations (model : Doubles Float64 <- doubles) : Bool := Doubles Float64 model

-- the computed distance `|a - b|` is a metric within a bound (D-2026-10-17-f; execution.md section
-- 4): the subtraction rounds once, `fl(a - b) = (a - b)(1 + delta)` with `|delta| <= u` (Higham
-- 2002, section 2.2, the standard model; exact when it falls below the normal range), and the
-- magnitude is exact, so the computed distance is within `u |a - b| <= u (|a| + |b|)` of the
-- exact one; `u = epsilon / 2` is the unit roundoff of the format (1.S9), not a criterion
absDistanceIsAMetricWithinModel : MetricWithin Float64 (structural Float64) :=
  { d := absDistance, bound := fn a b { doubles.epsilon / 2.0 * (abs a + abs b) } }
AbsDistanceIsAMetricWithin (model : MetricWithin Float64 (structural Float64) <-
    { d := absDistance, bound := fn a b { doubles.epsilon / 2.0 * (abs a + abs b) } }) : Bool :=
  MetricWithin Float64 (structural Float64) model
-- the claims of this file, gathered as core.lut's `CoreData` gathers the core's
NumberModels := DoublesHaveTheOperations & AbsDistanceIsAMetricWithin

-- the nearest double to an integer (11.S5)
conversion : Conversion := { convert := toFloat64 }

-- the elementary functions: leaves of the environment, declared over the doubles they receive
-- (11.S2), routes to Python's `math`, certified against numbers.lth §5 like any extern. Their
-- accuracy, `ulps`, is one unit in the last place [proposed, wave C bite C1, 2026-10-25]: measured
-- at most 0.502 for `exp` and 0.500 for `log`, `erf` and `sqrt` on this host's library (Debian
-- GLIBC 2.43, record/plan/audit-algebras-2026-10-22/c1-ulps.py, seed 1); one is the bound glibc's
-- manual table of known maximum errors is to be cited for, and the laws' certificates show how much
-- of it the host uses (the worst ratio).
elementary : Elementary := {
  sqrt := extern "python:math.sqrt",
  exp := extern "python:math.exp",
  log := extern "python:math.log",
  erf := extern "python:math.erf",
  ulps := 1.0,
}
